#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7498df7d-a340-599e-b4ba-496f5142c28c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12379" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the cone $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ that is subject to the constraints  $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4,\\, x\\geq 0$ and $y\\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2+y^2\\leq 4, \\, x\\geq 0, \\, y\\geq 0 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be8246dc3bfe1bf16af5d9913fc4e6aef58ddad282646a00d3e31120f6bf31e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175829 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/id #uuid "bcdc7b9f-b5f8-5516-a0e6-8ca54fb80a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8948" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x =y+\\sqrt 2 z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1 \\leq y\\leq 2$ and $1 \\leq z \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx = y+\\sqrt 2 z, \\qquad (y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/24/240ac546b0dd725e392dd0bd475b2ef00eebce2594be5921285e227137538c46.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "80c2abda-a1d9-569e-936e-fc44ba77eb50" :question/math-academy-id "e-8959" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = y - 4x$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z = f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = y - 4x, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4x)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4x)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+16+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{18}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175848 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "77e8fb3e-1858-5831-b740-a0c133c8180f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9141" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the plane $z = 2y+2x$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\cos{x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d609a1288446f0f3adc0de65b814f2a6daaf2d398478e7834882f3be68c8c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+2x)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+2x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9badee52-54da-5532-a93d-0f05f788bef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + x^{2} + y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173525} {:answer/id #uuid "e03cfe50-c522-5fe9-b13e-5a5d7ef7f664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - x^{2} - y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173526} {:answer/id #uuid "fef8a065-ae55-523a-9e77-0483d03a6d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1 + x^{2}y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173527} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca0f7c6-5f76-5dd8-b8b6-d62dc02dc6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + x^{2}y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173528} {:answer/id #uuid "202c2fb3-b058-5058-b467-ebfe075a185e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173529}] :answer-field/id #uuid "40f25b6b-11c0-5f63-8728-912fd81b80c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173524}] :question/id #uuid "944c1671-bbab-513c-b727-a199c9a9429b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105936" :question/problem "$∬_(D)AAAAAAAAdA, D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le 2, 2 \\le y \\le 3}$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z = xy$ for $1 \\le x \\le 2$ and $2 \\le y \\le 3$. What is the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7bc11c-66f0-516c-be38-a48045d964f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238162} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74bf5b-a988-54bf-b10b-2eefe28915c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238601} {:answer/id #uuid "501240ae-b8a0-5acb-a0c9-930075c91b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238746} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3c513-d899-5100-8a5d-0ae0cc06c64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239723} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f99997-a927-59f4-93d4-69604382fa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f99997-a927-59f4-93d4-69604382fa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240247} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca857f1-1bc6-513d-8d2d-903414af839c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40e44b1f-1930-5622-81b2-291ea09677cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106034" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242455a6-be88-54f8-ad2a-073aed9219d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244630} {:answer/id #uuid "25680994-f4bd-54a1-a482-ab99f94a76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244645} {:answer/id #uuid "47ddc665-80e7-527f-a69c-522a305cffe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244954} {:answer/id #uuid "63c479b4-bb47-56ba-b47e-40fa34876d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245188} {:answer/id #uuid "d716a18a-4a0d-5bb7-af54-448947c53223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25680994-f4bd-54a1-a482-ab99f94a76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244645} :answer-field/id #uuid "7687ea4b-b2b9-5647-8d8a-b1fe78f2a22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9b2c67-e61f-5dae-a147-dc5eaf3565f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109594" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8abbdf4-19fd-51f5-b473-e1af1dc33c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}1 + x^{2} + y^{2}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173717} {:answer/id #uuid "e9acb167-2737-5291-b7b1-248245a96c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + x + y}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173718} {:answer/id #uuid "1bebd994-6a12-5b90-9430-60b042c9aaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{2} + 4y^{2}}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173719} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf61d90-9f9d-59a7-b655-e97ed6fc23bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 2x + 2y}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173720} {:answer/id #uuid "4f49bcd8-6138-581d-926e-bdbb06a64428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 2x^{2} + 2y^{2}}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173721}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47c77966-d76d-5dda-b825-f36361203c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a3e1b6-3e3a-58d9-b55e-673dc95b3342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386433} {:answer/id #uuid "74e07110-fee2-57d3-a642-c06c3a73ad70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386434} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3cfca1-5908-516d-b059-28896f2cda16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386435} {:answer/id #uuid "b36c6634-4001-5695-9ee5-b01ec38ac4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+2x+2y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386436} {:answer/id #uuid "78982c37-dd15-5cfc-a4b2-ec194bcb56a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3cfca1-5908-516d-b059-28896f2cda16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386435} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1582c5-7435-5ca4-898e-aba8007cc959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c85ae37-614c-52e2-a347-0d9aae387c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-109600" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45948700-503f-551b-bdea-87109566160f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276090} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab93a6e-5eac-5228-b299-b866a1ad303c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276135} {:answer/id #uuid "6e807a78-b474-5a7f-8440-4f35e51cb60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276468} {:answer/id #uuid "81b78249-6d6c-5a58-8ea3-ac7673969a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276646} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdcb59c-b7f0-5394-8e00-a4e1644424c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdcb59c-b7f0-5394-8e00-a4e1644424c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277554} :answer-field/id #uuid "69336c1f-3dc5-5f03-92c4-84bacf0d2e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "128c01a3-ffea-59eb-9892-8068500c0400" :question/math-academy-id "q-161126" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad4c485-991a-5037-b69d-883685454737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275727} {:answer/id #uuid "870ff709-7130-5f4a-824d-3df0deccd82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276691} {:answer/id #uuid "b05684f5-deb4-54a0-b913-186656656d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277055} {:answer/id #uuid "b76e9e2d-b3d1-5d08-a8a9-414b1e3a6f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277106} {:answer/id #uuid "e2342e6a-26fb-5edd-a09d-f48dbbcb61cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05684f5-deb4-54a0-b913-186656656d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277055} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ba9dfd-f03d-578b-831f-8a7946d17b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b62215b7-0e69-5d45-8968-8bb0a1bc12ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-161131" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1d4d80-59ab-5ead-8c78-767fd4b1e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275593} {:answer/id #uuid "35aef836-1879-55df-a3ee-bdb4fee77c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275956} {:answer/id #uuid "5552d5d8-21b2-5826-9b06-0d57a1ca45e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}2+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276235} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1b7425-0593-5bfc-8a0c-244f08247594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276285} {:answer/id #uuid "a43e0aa8-b0f3-53aa-a270-9f7badeb4b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1d4d80-59ab-5ead-8c78-767fd4b1e908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275593} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75d780b-e75e-5b25-86f0-f1f9572d8822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c11e996e-10b6-5010-bc4a-0d91257a3dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161139" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x+{y}^{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=1.$ Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/87/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d540b0-4408-5667-a0db-a90347a718b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389169} {:answer/id #uuid "37b76347-5859-5d6e-8b0f-ab07a22b98cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389170} {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389171} {:answer/id #uuid "86209031-deeb-5c6b-ae0a-144a5367c604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389172} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c61fd-3721-53ba-9d5b-95914b2da4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}1+{y}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23fcbc4-3abe-5fd3-bdce-8e4f9b6793af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc263672-04e3-59a9-9f18-ccb933eb5e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-161141" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=y{e}^{x}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=y{e}^{x},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/90/903b7f3c6bd9721a974160487ca5099fdf54ce02b61697a7830bb662b26d8953.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y{e}^{x})^{2}+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ddc779-96d9-5d16-aac4-0dabf1e17a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275502} {:answer/id #uuid "30e154fd-4497-5263-9b08-b6765b29c0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275911} {:answer/id #uuid "631f8d2b-2496-59aa-99b5-1012433451b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276375} {:answer/id #uuid "676c7ba3-971e-57ed-8e51-801a143984bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276403} {:answer/id #uuid "db0668ec-9a7d-5423-8d91-6e6280c3e7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631f8d2b-2496-59aa-99b5-1012433451b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a3e6e60-777f-5683-818b-00f5995e5b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbda2ff7-05a0-5600-96fa-c9dfe7d1924f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161156" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the cylinder $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1345328c-d6f4-52f6-a50a-dfaeca58fda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275654} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb82ad8-ecc6-50fb-a005-2657818a4003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276299} {:answer/id #uuid "89c7d3f9-cb05-5b6e-8f6c-3becc6ff3485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276722} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a9609e-3d6e-55a8-8aa4-446ec434eeb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi (\\sqrt{6}-1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276960} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e64f96-04c2-5753-bd3d-45634f0c1247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1345328c-d6f4-52f6-a50a-dfaeca58fda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275654} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b1140ff-6cec-5c9f-9667-884f9e1954ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd23390c-f0cc-59ec-891d-3ea7fdf2c7b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161164" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2y-x$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ and $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2y-x,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,x\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c6c556ec90138fa1f0b599c6afed9d093e5c1b10f7751a5fbe55f0add617e1d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135454e4-1120-519b-8902-0052c3683dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275655} {:answer/id #uuid "38f4f294-2474-5ce3-90de-838266743040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275980} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6fca20-67c7-5d8e-8d89-12c47dd52ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276171} {:answer/id #uuid "acf33122-dba0-5c13-9a3a-5b9491194170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277033} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d82ada-d288-5161-b0d8-ce0977b5f48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (3-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf33122-dba0-5c13-9a3a-5b9491194170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277033} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc831c5-e9a8-5859-af7d-581c65abc8a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ec1dd0-f28a-513c-a9f4-29ac282d0de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161167" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7cb4d16f39d1c1e38d65000471cbc5ca1831a0bbc2cfc61c4f03d087d6a5125.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=4-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r & =-\\pi {\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2{({u}^{1/2})}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({4}^{1/2}-{3}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe3a77c-800e-5f02-af28-ff75516852cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275615} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2092ad-7785-5787-b333-6d0fce8ca8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (\\sqrt{23}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275721} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc469de-7af1-5811-a2fa-a41c24f21c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276454} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f27509-b929-5918-967e-009c7bf1b045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (3\\sqrt{15}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277253} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6bc52-08f6-52ed-bfc8-71ce0195e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (17\\sqrt{15}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe3a77c-800e-5f02-af28-ff75516852cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275615} :answer-field/id #uuid "944c8782-8047-5b05-afcc-d1e7c99a7d62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5e36073-07ed-5be9-b9f9-14a5dbeadf43" :question/math-academy-id "q-161173" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $x={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9$ and $z\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9,\\,z\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd5bcd7857769afb5f3a4f6197757c08eb295c7075a271c302c4b6087d661d14.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2fe78b-4cd0-558d-9e93-31f26208dc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7778ed-117c-5fa1-acc1-b2c97b01b3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{3}+1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276018} {:answer/id #uuid "56775f02-8b2b-5019-9e46-91e136692e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276248} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6135d4-8f96-520b-8b96-abd5e27a8317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276890} {:answer/id #uuid "aca71121-a3e0-5db8-81bc-cf1fa06db8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2fe78b-4cd0-558d-9e93-31f26208dc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276017} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b61f550-2b34-5e06-841f-8c7efed4d644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d00a6aa-63ec-5be5-aa3c-4e85a0b4a1af" :question/math-academy-id "q-161174" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x=2-y-z$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=2-y-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/37/3741c5db05232f04f60f747dc04176bfc77da30bbc39c79888cf4c18939369f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-y-z)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2-y-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b69cd4c-0c3e-5f84-a667-3e998671dc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{13}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275871} {:answer/id #uuid "78922260-8fb0-5574-b9fe-40db1c7a986b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276570} {:answer/id #uuid "7d29f2a0-987c-5e06-bd1c-9d69b2646c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276618} {:answer/id #uuid "9909b8d8-f47f-5d7d-ae9b-ab6300fee337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276851} {:answer/id #uuid "fff26cfa-64e7-5943-9ce1-56d10da11f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9909b8d8-f47f-5d7d-ae9b-ab6300fee337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276851} :answer-field/id #uuid "e948b81b-2e80-5712-b122-75bcc52059e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fedbba32-290f-52d2-807e-9187e81e100a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161176" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab855ef2b9b5363005c14ea37470ed9ecb5853380a301341d20e2cd6095a5d1f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427080d-6e07-595f-8bdf-12b82df47007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275939} {:answer/id #uuid "42b74163-07b3-59fe-a577-429799e99107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}(\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276064} {:answer/id #uuid "658efc61-0551-5e6f-a3d1-14f7c8f8d9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{10}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276389} {:answer/id #uuid "d57fe3a4-c6d8-5413-a5a4-03161fc89524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277356} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c185fa-40eb-5867-a9e7-4b8abc10c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c185fa-40eb-5867-a9e7-4b8abc10c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277600} :answer-field/id #uuid "96482818-83a7-5e7b-bd3f-117b12c337e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dce2ffe-4326-5f45-948b-458dac5baa92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161177" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\sqrt{x},$ $x=4,$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a4ed826b683e7e8d8ed3e05bcb75618e74314febcdda8ae31b09f379ca21c73.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)={x}^{3/4}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7/4}+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{3/4})}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{x}^{3/2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 4 \\\\ u & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{2+{x}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{9}(5\\sqrt{5}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc235a2-69a7-56fd-ab60-88369ca3597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(e+2)^{3/2}+9\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275769} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecb222f-5eff-5885-bd05-541e193d3731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\sqrt{3}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276470} {:answer/id #uuid "7a209b18-88fa-51c4-94f1-1a2189c18e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(e-1)^{3/2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276586} {:answer/id #uuid "99ae597c-3a2e-53b6-a512-8da9aede5125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e+2}-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276856} {:answer/id #uuid "cd431321-0991-589f-8644-2625a8488766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd431321-0991-589f-8644-2625a8488766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277300} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8c0ead-ac6b-5ddf-9490-787f763c2907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c338dfd1-e7f8-59f7-ac67-821e4b45c353" :question/math-academy-id "q-161214" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={e}^{y}-z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z={e}^{2y},$ $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{2y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9c/9cae5b68f77c2d79df8a81d0421146ac2bbe618c6065a457c33c86caceeb1327.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}-z)={e}^{y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{y})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{2y}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z={e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u={e}^{2y}+2,$ we get $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ u & 3 & e+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{e+2}\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((e+2)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1735e166-5acc-5324-851d-91faeb7a9618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415891} {:answer/id #uuid "3369dcb6-e91a-5e4b-b5d1-596236417674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16(x+y)}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,y\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415895} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba101ca-c17d-55ee-8ca8-392a0b3b4fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}<4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415900} {:answer/id #uuid "d803556f-449c-5597-ae86-ae738f26a508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and $\\,y\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415920} {:answer/id #uuid "f8918b25-32d9-5a19-ba97-973014cfd089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8918b25-32d9-5a19-ba97-973014cfd089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415926} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2b5a8a-3d1e-588b-a0ea-de39882b51aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d2fc887-f2a2-5a12-af93-46ac7487f8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161442" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the cone $y=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}})=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}})=\\frac{4z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{16{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}+\\frac{16{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{17({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{17}\\,$ and $\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02096d36-ef67-5df8-a1fc-77f61f4fb7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275495} {:answer/id #uuid "0890c454-a982-5e24-83e4-ccc175ff3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275553} {:answer/id #uuid "c58299d5-6fa2-5896-8f16-ff7a78529fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277233} {:answer/id #uuid "df5bf9db-65c0-5560-b70c-3b38da48a945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277431} {:answer/id #uuid "e618a500-fbdb-51bb-b479-a405e95937de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{2}{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df5bf9db-65c0-5560-b70c-3b38da48a945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277431} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ee5d51-e4ff-5d64-b516-e18fbea43931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e50892b-6dbf-5e7c-ae2e-f87a4a349f42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161443" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{1}{xz}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{xz},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}{z}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$"}]}
